turnip (guamp A610): the per-overlap flush only for the pipelines that read their colour attachments (TU_GUAMP_FLOOP, default on)

metal2vulkan reads Metal framebuffer fetch as input attachments in rasterization order: in such a pass every colour attachment is
also an input attachment of the subpass, and only the fetching pipelines carry RASTERIZATION_ORDER_ATTACHMENT_ACCESS. Turnip
(tu_render_pass_check_feedback_loop) then marks the whole subpass as a colour feedback loop and gives EVERY pipeline of the pass
COLOR_ATTACHMENT_FEEDBACK_LOOP, so tu6_emit_prim_mode_sysmem drew every draw with GRAS_SC_CNTL FLUSH_PER_OVERLAP_AND_OVERWRITE (CCU
written back to DRAM at each overlap): fin55 TU row f31495 of s36932 Turnip passes for 31514 bridge passes, i.e. every pass, every
draw. The cost grows with overlapping layers, not with blurs (user: the flat ringtone list scrolls smoothly, Reduce Transparency,
which adds opaque layers over the same pixels, is slower).
Now a pipeline that reads no input attachment (new tu_shader fs.guamp_reads_inputs, set when the FS binds one) and has no
rasterization-order access drops a colour-only feedback loop (NO_FLUSH); before the first draw of a pipeline that does read after
one that did not (or of one with rasterization-order access), the render pass cache gets CCU_CLEAN_COLOR + CACHE_INVALIDATE + WAIT_FOR_IDLE (what a by-region self-dependency
barrier emits). The fetching pipelines keep FLUSH_PER_OVERLAP_AND_OVERWRITE (sysmem) / FLUSH_PER_OVERLAP (GMEM). Depth/stencil
feedback loops, dynamic rendering and the UBWC rule for readers are unchanged. rp.sysmem_single_prim_mode (TU row f, autotune GMEM
choice) now marks only passes that really fetch. TU_GUAMP_FLOOP=0|off restores the upstream rule. Applies after 0001-0004.

